{
 "summary": "The paper introduces Prefix-conditioned Supervised Fine-Tuning (P-SFT) to address diversity collapse in large language models during training and inference. By assigning semantically neutral prefixes to prompts, P-SFT decouples multiple responses, mitigating gradient conflicts and enabling more diverse outputs. The method is evaluated on diverse reasoning and creativity tasks, showing significant improvements in diversity and performance. The approach is also shown to enhance test-time scaling and serve as a better initialization for reinforcement learning.",
 "strengths": [
  "Identifies a novel source of diversity collapse during SFT and proposes a conceptually simple but effective solution.",
  "Empirical validation is thorough and covers a wide range of reasoning and creativity tasks, including comparisons with strong baselines and ablation studies.",
  "The method is easy to implement and can be applied during both training and inference without parameter updates, making it highly practical.",
  "The paper provides theoretical insights into how prefixing affects model training and inference, supported by empirical metrics like KL divergence and cosine similarity."
],
 "weaknesses": [
  {
    "issue": "The paper does not provide a comprehensive comparison with methods that directly address diversity during reinforcement learning, such as DAPO or entropy regularization, which could have better contextualized the novelty of P-SFT.",
    "location": "related work",
    "severity": "major"
  },
  {
    "issue": "The paper does not clarify the computational cost of P-SFT training or inference compared to traditional SFT, which is important for assessing practicality in real-world deployment.",
    "location": "experiments",
    "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
    "issue": "Some ablation studies (e.g., prefix types) are not sufficiently explained in terms of why they were chosen or how they reflect broader design principles for latent perturbation.",
    "location": "4.4",
    "severity": "minor"
  }
],
